**2. Wire it up in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register Problem Details + custom handler
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ExceptionToProblemDetailsHandler>();

var app = builder.Build();

// Must be early in the pipeline
app.UseExceptionHandler();

// Your endpoints — no try-catch needed
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive.");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found.");
    if (id == 99) throw new InvalidOperationException("Item is archived.");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Widget" });
});

app.Run();
```

**How it works:**

| Exception | Status | Detail shown? |
|-----------|--------|---------------|
| `KeyNotFoundException` | 404 | Yes (message) |
| `ArgumentException` | 400 | Yes (message) |
| `InvalidOperationException` | 409 | Yes (message) |
| Anything else | 500 | Generic only |

**Key points:**
- `IExceptionHandler` (added in .NET 8) integrates with the built-in exception handler middleware — no custom middleware needed.
- The 500 case deliberately hides `exception.Message` to avoid leaking internals.
- Returning `true` from `TryHandleAsync` signals the exception is fully handled.
- You can register multiple `IExceptionHandler` implementations; they run in order until one returns `true`.
